<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Jun 29, 2022">
<CPscript title="Conformal Mapping I" >
<text> This script explains what discrete conformal mappings are and
illustrates the two basic approaches to their construction. </text>
<Section title="Definition">
<text> A (discrete) conformal mapping is a mapping f: P --> Q between 
two univalent circle packings sharing the same combinatorics K.
A circle packing is "univalent" if its circles have mutually
disjoint interiors. If P and Q are two univalent circle packings 
with the same combinatorics, then the map f identifying each 
circle of P with the corresponding circle of Q is a conformal
mapping. Here's an elementary example. </text>
<cmd iconname="centrejust.png">act 0;infile_read simpleP.p;set_screen -d;disp -w -c;copy 1; act 1;infile_read simpleQ.p;set_screen -d;disp -w -c;Map 0 1 -o;act 0; </cmd>
<text> Note that K is a very simple complex; you can click on a circle in
one of the packings and the corresponding circle will appear in 
the other.  
These packings were easy to generate by ad hoc methods; in fact,
you can modify these easily by increasing or decreasing some 
boundary circles. The next command makes minor randomized 
adjustments to boundary radii in P and the following command 
carries out the repacking (there's no guarantee that the result
will be univalent). You can repeat the two command by putting
the mouse in the window and pressing 'a' (for adjust) and then 
'r' (for repack). (You can also make changes to boundary radii
with the mouse in the 'increase' or 'decrease' radius mode.) </text>
<cmd name="a" mnemonic="a" iconname="dnd_multi.png">act 0;Map -x;set_random -f -r .8 1.2 b;disp -c b; </cmd>
<cmd name="r" mnemonic="r" iconname="psi.png">act 0;repack;layout;disp -w -c; </cmd>
<text> Often a canonical choice is made for the domain packing P -- namely,
the maximal packing for K in the unit disc. The map f: P --> Q is
then the discrete analogue of a classical conformal mapping of the
disc onto a plane region (i.e., Q). </text>
<cmd iconname="xeyes.png">act 0;max_pack;set_screen -d;disp -w -c; </cmd>
<text> As a final observation, the mapping f may be interpreted as a
mapping from the carrier of P to the carrier of Q, as shown next. </text>
<cmd iconname="kivio.png">disp -p0 -w -ff;disp -p1 -w -ff; </cmd>
<text> There is a rather extreme "discretization" effect due to the small
size of the packing K in this example, but when the circle packings
are more complex, the carrier of P come closer to filling out the
unit disc and that of Q comes closer to defining an open region of
the plane. We will see examples in the next sections. </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Cookie Construction">
<text> There are two basic methods of creating nontrivial discrete 
conformal mappings. This first one creates a packing in a
target region Omega in the plane by cookie-cutting it from a larger 
euclidean packing. (This is the construction suggested by William 
Thurston in 1985 when he got this topic started.)
Here is the curve defining Omega (as one would draw it with the
mouse, for example) and a superimposed hexagonal circle packing. </text>
<cmd iconname="kuickshow.png">Map -x;act 1; set_screen -d; infile_path test_curve.g; seed -q ;geom_to_e;add_gen 20 6;set_rad .04 a;layout; disp -w -c -g; </cmd>
<text> We execute a cookie-cut; this gives a packing roughly approximating
Omega. </text>
<cmd iconname="mozilla.png">act 1;cookie;disp -w -c -g; </cmd>
<text> It also gives us the complex K of that packing, as illustrated
if we draw in the carrier. </text>
<cmd iconname="netbeans.png">act 1;disp -f; </cmd>
<text> Sending a copy of the packing in p1 to p0, we create the domain
by converting p0 to hyperbolic geometry, setting the boundary radii
to infinite hyperbolic radius, repacking, and laying out for the
maximal packing. </text>
<cmd iconname="klines.png">copy -p1 0;act 0;geom_to_h;set_rad -.1 b;repack;layout; disp -w -c; </cmd>
<text> Here, then, is the resulting conformal mapping. </text>
<cmd iconname="kghostview.png">disp -p0 -cf b;disp -p1 -cf b;Map 0 1 -o; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Boundary Angle Sums">
<text> Our second method for creating conformal mappings starts with a 
maximal packing as domain in p0 and creates a euclidean range 
packing by prescribing boundary angle sums. In this example, we 
use a packing with rather random combinatorics. </text>
<cmd iconname="amarok.png">Map -x;act 0;infile_read misc_579.p;disp -w -c; </cmd>
<text> Four boundary vertices are identified. </text>
<cmd iconname="apollon.png">set_Vlist 499 514 527 547;disp -cf Vlist; </cmd>
<text> We copy this packing to p1 and convert it to euclidean geometry; you
will not really see any difference, but note that we have displayed
the boundary edges. </text>
<cmd iconname="userconfig.png">copy -p0 1;act 1;geom_to_e;disp -w -c -e b -cf Vlist; </cmd>
<text> Here we prescribe the boundary angle sums: namely, the angles formed
by the boundary edges at each boundary vertex. We set these to be
Pi at every boundary vertex, then reset those at the specified
four vertices to Pi/2. When we repack, we get the image packing
with its carrier equal forming a rectangle. </text>
<cmd iconname="metacontact_online.png">set_aim 1.0 b;set_aim .5 Vlist;repack 1000;layout; disp -w -c -e b -cf Vlist; </cmd>
<text> The size and orientation can be adjusted to get a better view. </text>
<cmd iconname="icq_dnd.png">norm_scale -h Vlist[1] Vlist[2];norm_scale -u Vlist[1];set_screen -a; disp -w -c -e b -cf Vlist;Map 0 1 -o; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Further reading">
<text> Both methods here can be used to create discrete conformal mappings
which mimic and approximate classical conformal mappings. The key,
of course is to build the univalent packings in the range. The script
Conformal_Mapping_II.xmd illustates 'cookie' methods for muliply 
connected plane regions, while Conformal_Mapping_III.xmd discusses
conformal mapping for non-planar domains, and Riemann surfaces. </text>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
<CPdata>
